    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you how proof by induction works and why it is valid. Talk through the reasoning; you will not need to write anything down. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Mathematical induction proves that a statement P(n) is true for every natural number n from some starting value onward. It has two steps. The base case shows that P holds for the first value, usually n = 0 or n = 1. The inductive step shows that for every k, if P(k) is true then P(k + 1) is true.",
      "The assumption that P(k) holds in the inductive step is called the inductive hypothesis. It is not circular: we do not assume what we want to prove for all n, only that it holds for one arbitrary k, and we show that truth carries forward from k to k + 1.",
      "Induction is often compared to a line of dominoes. The base case knocks over the first domino; the inductive step guarantees that each falling domino knocks over the next; so every domino falls. Its validity rests on the well-ordering of the natural numbers: if some n failed, there would be a smallest failure, and the inductive step from the number before it would be contradicted.",
      "A classic example is that 1 + 2 + ... + n equals n(n + 1)/2. Both steps are needed. A statement can hold for the first million cases and still fail later, so checking many examples is not a proof; and an inductive step without a true base case proves nothing, since the chain never starts. Strong induction lets the inductive step assume P for all values up to k, not only k itself."
    ],
