        "facts": ["The base case proves the first value.", "The inductive step proves P(k) implies P(k + 1).", "Together they prove P for all values from the start onward."],
        "misconceptions": ["The inductive step alone is enough."] },
      { "key": "hypothesis", "title": "Explain why the inductive hypothesis is not circular", "description": "Distinguishes assuming P(k) for one k from assuming the whole claim.",
        "concept": "Inductive hypothesis", "definition": "The assumption that P(k) holds for an arbitrary k, used to prove P(k + 1).",
        "facts": ["The hypothesis is about one arbitrary k.", "The step proves an implication, not the statement itself.", "It does not assume the result for all n."],
        "misconceptions": ["Induction assumes what it is trying to prove."] },
      { "key": "validity", "title": "Explain why induction is valid", "description": "Uses the domino picture or well-ordering to justify the method.",
        "concept": "Validity of induction", "definition": "Induction is valid because the natural numbers are well-ordered: a smallest counterexample would contradict the steps.",
        "facts": ["Each true case forces the next to be true.", "A smallest counterexample would contradict the inductive step or the base case.", "Strong induction may assume all earlier cases."],
        "misconceptions": ["Induction is a form of guessing from patterns."] },
      { "key": "examples-not-proof", "title": "Explain why examples are not a proof", "description": "Explains why many checked cases do not prove a statement for all n.",
        "concept": "Examples versus proof", "definition": "Checking finitely many cases cannot rule out a later counterexample; a proof covers every case.",
