    "category": "mathematics",
    "subject": "Discrete Mathematics",
    "topic": "Proof techniques",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you how proof by contradiction works, using classic examples. Explain each step of the argument aloud; you will not need to write anything down. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "A proof by contradiction establishes a statement by assuming that it is false and showing that this assumption leads to something impossible, such as a claim that is both true and false. Since a correct chain of reasoning from a true starting point cannot reach a contradiction, the assumption must be false, so the original statement is true.",
      "The square root of 2 is irrational. Suppose instead that it equals a fraction p/q in lowest terms. Squaring gives p squared = 2 q squared, so p squared is even, and therefore p is even. Writing p = 2r gives q squared = 2 r squared, so q is even too. Then p and q share the factor 2, contradicting lowest terms. So no such fraction exists.",
      "Euclid proved that there are infinitely many primes. Suppose there were only finitely many, and multiply them all together and add 1. The resulting number leaves remainder 1 when divided by any prime on the list, so none of them divides it. But every whole number greater than 1 has a prime factor, so there is a prime not on the list, contradicting the assumption that the list was complete.",
      "Proof by contradiction differs from proof by contrapositive. To prove 'if A then B', the contrapositive method proves 'if not B then not A', which is logically equivalent. A contradiction proof instead assumes A and not B together and derives any impossibility. Note that Euclid's number need not itself be prime; it only has a prime factor missing from the list."
    ],
