  {
    "title": "Basic Probability",
    "category": "mathematics",
    "subject": "Probability",
    "topic": "Probability rules",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you about sample spaces, independence and conditional probability. Explain your reasoning with everyday examples such as coins, dice and cards; you will not need to write anything down. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "A probability experiment has a sample space, the set of all possible outcomes, and an event is a subset of it. Every probability lies between 0 and 1, and the probabilities of all outcomes add to 1. When outcomes are equally likely, the probability of an event is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number: a fair die shows an even number with probability 3/6 = 1/2.",
