      "So there are different sizes of infinity: the reals are strictly larger than the natural numbers. For infinite sets, being a proper subset does not make a set smaller, which is why size is defined by pairing rather than by containment. Cantor's theorem goes further: the set of all subsets of any set is always strictly larger than the set itself, so there is no largest infinity."
